        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Does it pop up with `bar=` as the first completion candidate after typing `ba`?\n\nYes, it reproduces this. I also managed to find a simple example of using `@dataclass` and it also reproduced the issue.\n\nThe culprit here is kind of both 'mini.completion' and `pyright`. The completion candidate for the `bar=` case has the following structure:\n\n```\ndata = {\n  position = { line = 3, character = 6 },\n  uri = \"file:///path/to/directory/tmp.py\"\n},\nfilterText = \"bar\",\nkind = 6,\nlabel = \"bar=\",\nsortText = \"04.9999.bar\"\n```\n\nThe problem lies in the fact that it only contains `label` and `filterText`. In this case 'mini.completion' prefers `filterText` as the  text that will be inserted. The reason is kind of technical plus it makes it work better for *other* languages. For reference, see these: 9af69d8c655e609a7eb043e8f9c27530580d4838 and #1721. The built-in omnifunc doesn't use `filterText` in this particular case, so there is no such behavior there.\n\nThere would be no problem if `pyright` followed the LSP specification a bit more and provide explicitly which text it wants to insert. As using `label` as the text to insert is kind of not good.\n\nLuckily, there is a solution here by adjusting how items are processed specifically in Python files. In particular, create a '~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/python.lua' file and add the following lines into it:\n\n```lua\nvim.b.minicompletion_config = {\n  lsp_completion = {\n    process_items = function(items, base)\n      for _, item in ipairs(items) do\n        item.insertText = item.insertText or item.label\n      end\n      return MiniCompletion.default_process_items(items, base)\n    end\n  }\n}\n```",
